From 4db851e4263f41f266da8899dbfc9cedcef07c23 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BRIGHT evolution experiment <experiment@local>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 20:57:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] aegis/tools: make Bash work on Windows and add a real
 per-stage cost cap

Two independent blockers, both found by reading what the meta-agents actually did.

1. Bash was dead on Windows. _WORK_ROOT defaulted to the literal
   '/tmp/harnessx-work', which on Windows is a drive-relative '\\tmp\\...' that
   needs admin rights, so _get_work_dir() raised WinError 5 before any command
   ran. Every Bash call in the BRIGHT pilot failed, so the Evolver could not run
   the mandatory build->verify loop for new code and fell back to prompt-only
   candidates -- silently disabling the tools and processor evolution buckets.
   Fixed with a cross-platform default plus the existing HARNESSX_WORK_DIR
   override.

   Then the process still would not start: asyncio's Windows subprocess transport
   is built on NAMED pipes, which some sandboxes forbid (bare WinError 5), while
   subprocess.run() with anonymous pipes works. The blocking call now runs in a
   worker thread. A shell probe (HARNESSX_BASH) prefers a real bash and falls
   back to the platform default when MSYS2's signal pipe is unavailable.

2. Cost had no effective brake. CostGuardProcessor ceilings were hard-coded to
   \ per stage at four sites; the BRIGHT pilot spent \.5 in one round
   (Evolver alone \.32 / 4.9M tokens / 63 steps). All sites now read
   harnessx/aegis/budget.py: AEGIS_MAX_STAGE_COST_USD.
   Note AegisAgent.budget_per_round_usd is accepted but never read, so it
   enforces nothing.
